  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Agroecological agriculture and inclusive resource management in Dekese/Kasai-DRC</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The action aims to support vulnerable farming households by strengthening their sustainable food systems and resilience. This will be achieved by developing environmentally -friendly agroecological practices to ensure food and nutritional security. In addition, the action will promote the development of value chains adapted to the specific context, in order to create wealth in an inclusive manner. The aim is to improve agricultural productivity, the socio -economic resilience of local populations, access to nutritious and sustainable food, and the sustainable management and conservation of natural resources. The project will deliver the following outputs 1) improve access for vulnerable households to agricultural inputs and improved knowledge of agroecology, as well as 2) raise awareness among local populations of environmental and biodiversity management and protection. 3) facilitate the access of target groups to the network of key economic players in the local agricultural sector, and 4) improve the coordination of their actions, as well as 5) enhance the value of agricultural production and the adaptation of value chains to the local economy. The project targets 6,000 households, including around 3,800 in Dekese, 1,500 in Kole and 700 in Oshwe, i.e. 36,000 of the most vulnerable people (5,832 men, 1,3608 women, 16,560 children). The project will be implemented in collaboration with: 1. the Local Development Committees (LDC); 2. the Rural Agricultural Management Council (CARG); 3. the Provincial and Territorial Inspectorates of Agriculture (IPA) of Dekese, the Territorial Inspectorate of Fisheries and Livestock, and the Territorial Inspectorate of Rural Development will also play an active role in the training of CARG agents. The development of synergies will be strengthened with local agricultural development players, namely :World Wild Fund (WWF), Institut Congolais pour la Conservation de la nature - Congolese Institute for Nature Conversation of the Nature (ICCN</narrative>
  </description>
